  "prompt_text": "The figure illustrates how bending creates strain in a thin electronic device and how packaging layers can worsen mechanical failure. In the simpler structure on the left, a blue device sits on a substrate. When the stack is bent, the top surface is stretched and the lower part is compressed, shown by arrows pointing in opposite directions. A dashed line marks the neutral strain position, where strain is minimal; it lies within the substrate rather than in the device, so the device experiences tensile strain.\n\nThe right side shows a more realistic multilayer package with adhesive and encapsulation added above and below the device/substrate region. These extra layers increase the thickness and shift the strain distribution through the stack. The arrows indicate larger tensile strain near the outer top layers and stronger compressive strain toward the lower layers during bending. Because the outer encapsulation is far from the neutral axis, it appears especially vulnerable to cracking or rupture, while interfaces within the stack are prone to delamination. Overall, the figure conveys that encapsulation and adhesive layers can significantly change the neutral axis and amplify mechanical stress, increasing the risk of damage in flexible electronics.",
